*Interpretation:* baseline already solved these cases, creating a ceiling. Identical counts are not proof of equivalence, safety or production effectiveness. Results within a batch are correlated, not independent replications.

All four arms used two fresh worker stages with the same optional-check budget. Only the source-first arm received the author narrative after its provisional record; the other arms received it earlier. This exercised controlled information delivery through a relay, not reduced anchoring in one continuing reviewer.

The baseline was a simplified instruction, not the complete operational workflow. Cases were conspicuous development examples, order was fixed and each arm ran once; the combined A+B+C workflow was not a fifth comparative treatment. Exact model/version, token use and billed cost were unobservable, so elapsed job time does not establish efficiency.

== Research-ideation comparison

Two fresh workers applied the pre-edit or revised entry guidance to the same assurance brief, a laboratory-assay brief and an inaccessible-source control. Both avoided checksum-centered proposals, retained CFU/mL, dilution and detection-limit information, and disclosed the unavailable source. The revised response made causal links more explicit, but that is not a demonstrated gain in ideation quality.

Both responses overstated some causal mechanisms and introduced unvalidated decision thresholds. The revised response also exceeded the 1,100-word target: 1,236 whitespace words versus 1,099 before. The guard is an explicit instruction change, not a guarantee that an agent will follow it or that first-principles reasoning will succeed.

This comparison used one response per guidance version and tested the entry guidance rather than the full composed workflow. It did not validate proposed biological mechanisms or run a wet-laboratory experiment. No laboratory or aviation result should be inferred from these fictional transfer briefs.

== How to use these findings

- *Retain uncertainty:* describe the practices as implemented prototypes, not proven improvements.
- *Measure real use:* after the next one or two uses, inspect missed requirements, unjustified changes, accepted defects, clean-task blocking and observable review effort in existing receipts.
- *Preserve fair comparisons:* use genuinely new tasks, fixed answer keys and matched budgets; do not reinterpret correlated cases as independent evidence.
- *Prefer less machinery:* simplify or remove practices whose overhead is not justified; a null result is not permission to manufacture a positive one.

This guide preserves the designs, aggregate observations and limitations needed to interpret the work. Superseded narratives, fixture dumps and exact execution receipts are retained in task-private research records rather than the reader-facing documentation. Those records are not needed to follow this guide; the PDF alone is not a complete reproducibility package.
